c. Zakaria represented India abroad at the United Nations thrice. He was the first Muslim urban development minister of the newly created Maharashtra State. Later, he served as deputy to Former Prime Minister Indira Gandhi, the leader of the Congress Party, in the Lok Sabha. He was closely associated with the Indian independence movement and the Indian National Congress party.

He was known for his advocacy of traditional Islam. Rafiq Zakaria was a political leaders from Nala Sopara (1920-04-05, Nala Sopara–2005-07-09). Holds notable historical significance, documented across 0 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to political systems and geopolitics.", "newValue": "Rafiq Zakaria (1920–2005) was a prominent Indian politician, Islamic scholar, and author whose career spanned the critical decades of post-independence India, from the nationalist movement to the early 21st century. Born in Nala Sopara, Maharashtra, he emerged as a significant voice within the Indian National Congress, advocating for secular governance while simultaneously championing an informed understanding of traditional Islam in a modernizing nation. His influence extended from state-level administration in Maharashtra to national parliamentary politics and international diplomacy, representing India at the United Nations on three occasions, thereby shaping India's domestic policy and global image.

